+# Signet ML-DSA-44 block signatures (`-signetpqblock`)
+
+A custom signet can require, from a height on, that every block carries an
+ML-DSA-44 (FIPS 204) signature by a fixed public key, in addition to the
+signet solution its challenge already requires. Nodes without the rule keep
+accepting those blocks: the rule only narrows what is valid, so a signet can
+switch it on at a height without splitting from nodes that did not upgrade.
+
+This is an experiment of the Ark-0 test network. It is not part of BIP 325 or
+any other BIP.
+
+## What it proves and what it does not
+
+From the height on, a node with the rule accepts a block only if an ML-DSA-44
+signature by the configured key covers the block's contents: its version,
+previous block, time, every transaction, and the whole coinbase except the
+two pushes described below. A party that holds only the classical challenge
+key cannot make such a node accept a block with contents of its own.
+
+It does not make anything else post-quantum. Transaction outputs, proof of
+work and nodes without the rule are not covered.
+
+Like a signet solution, the signature does not cover `nNonce` and `nBits`. It
+does not cover the signet solution push either. So anyone can make blocks with
+the same contents and other hashes:
+
+- by grinding the block again;
+- by changing the solution in any way the challenge still accepts, such as
+  extra pushes in its scriptSig, which signet does not forbid.
+
+A holder of the classical key can also sign the block again.
+
+## Configuration
+
+    -signetpqblock=<pubkey>@<height>
+
+`<pubkey>` is the 1312-byte ML-DSA-44 public key in hex and `<height>` the
+first height that must carry a signature (at least 1). The option requires
+`-signetchallenge`, and every node of the network must use the same value
+before the chain reaches `<height>`. At startup a node logs the height and the
+SHA256 of the key, so that operators can compare them.
+
+The Ark-0 signet (challenge
+`5121026fc5d8e79a9fbc8bc3dea07d82641d16717ce0b000c13c512d8e8c1788c6e5da51ae`)
+refuses the option: its rules are fixed in the code.
+
+## The block
+
+At or above the height, the coinbase output that holds the witness commitment
+(the last one that starts with `OP_RETURN` and the 36-byte commitment push)
+must be exactly:
+
+| Order | Content | Encoding |
+|---|---|---|
+| 1 | `OP_RETURN` | `6a` |
+| 2 | witness commitment `aa21a9ed ‖ 32 bytes` | `24 ‖ 36 bytes` |
+| 3 | PQ push: `PQB1` (`50514231`) ‖ version `01` ‖ signature (2420 bytes), 2425 bytes | `4d 7909 ‖ 2425 bytes` |
+| 4 (optional) | signet solution push: `ecc7daa2` ‖ solution, longer than 4 bytes | shortest encoding |
+
+Nothing may follow. Any other form is rejected with `bad-signet-pqblk`.
+
+## The message
+
+1. Take the coinbase and replace the witness commitment output's script by
+   `6a ‖ 24 ‖ <36-byte commitment> ‖ 05 ‖ 50514231 01`: the PQ push cut back to
+   its header and version, the signet solution push left out.
+2. Compute the merkle root over that coinbase's txid and the txids of the
+   block's other transactions.
+3. The message is the 32-byte
+   `TaggedHash("SignetPQBlock", SHA256(challenge) ‖ nVersion ‖ hashPrevBlock ‖ merkle root ‖ nTime)`,
+   where `SHA256(challenge)` hashes the raw challenge bytes, `nVersion` is a
+   little-endian int32, `nTime` a little-endian uint32, and the two hashes are
+   32 bytes each in their serialized (internal) byte order.
+4. The signature is pure ML-DSA-44 over that message, with the context string
+   `SIGNET/PQBLOCK/EXP0` (FIPS 204 `ML-DSA.Verify`, external interface).
+
+## Where it is checked
+
+In `CheckBlock`, on the same condition as the signet solution (`fCheckPOW`),
+after the merkle root and the other context-free checks:
+
+- `ProcessNewBlock` and `AcceptBlock` check every new block.
+- `ConnectBlock` runs `CheckBlock` again for every block it connects,
+  `-reindex-chainstate` included.
+- `TestBlockValidity` checks templates and proposals without `fCheckPOW`, so
+  `getblocktemplate` keeps working at and above the height, as it does for
+  the signet solution.
+
+The height is the one the coinbase starts with (BIP34), in exactly the form
+`CScript() << height`. A coinbase without it counts as having reached the
+height; `ContextualCheckBlock` rejects such a block anyway.
+
+The genesis block is exempt, as it is from the signet solution. It has no
+witness commitment, and its coinbase starts with a push that reads as a height
+above any, but every node connects it at its first start.
+
+Below the height nothing is rejected. With `-debug=validation` a node logs for
+every block below the height but the genesis block whether it would pass: its
+signature valid, invalid, or absent or not in the required form. So the
+signatures can be checked before they are enforced.
+
+## Nodes that upgrade late
+
+A node that accepted blocks above the height before it had the rule does not
+check them again on its own. On restart, `-checkblocks` (default 6) checks the
+most recent ones and reports a corrupted block database if one fails. Run the
+node once with `-reindex-chainstate` to check every block again (a pruned node
+needs `-reindex`).
+
+A node with the rule treats a peer that relays a block without a valid
+signature as it treats any peer relaying an invalid block.
+
+## Producing blocks
+
+1. Build the block from `getblocktemplate` with its witness commitment.
+2. Append the push `05 50514231 01` to the witness commitment output.
+3. Compute the message, sign it, and replace that push by the full PQ push.
+4. Recompute the merkle root.
+5. Add the signet solution as usual (it now signs over the PQ push), recompute
+   the merkle root, and grind the nonce.
+
+The coinbase is about 9.7k weight units larger: start the node that makes
+templates with `-blockreservedweight=16000` or more.
+
+`test/functional/test_framework/signet_pq.py` implements steps 2 to 4;
+`test/util/generate_signet_pqblock_tests.py` generates the unit test vectors.
